<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!-- English (United States) language resources for OpenTheWindows.admx. -->
<policyDefinitionResources revision="1.0" schemaVersion="1.0"
                           xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/GroupPolicy/2006/07/PolicyDefinitions">
  <displayName>Open the Windows administrative template</displayName>
  <description>Group Policy settings for the Open the Windows system utility.</description>
  <resources>
    <stringTable>
      <string id="Cat_OpenTheWindows">Open the Windows</string>
      <string id="SUPPORTED_OpenTheWindows_1">Open the Windows 1.0 or later</string>
      <string id="RequireSignedProfiles">Require signed profiles</string>
      <string id="RequireSignedProfiles_Help">Controls whether Open the Windows will use a profile supplied as a file (otw scan/apply --profile &lt;path&gt;) only when it carries a trusted signature.

When this policy is Enabled, otw refuses to scan or apply a file profile unless a detached signature (&lt;file&gt;.sig) accompanies it and verifies against a public key in the machine trust store at %ProgramData%\OpenTheWindows\trusted-keys\*.pem. Unsigned or untrusted file profiles are rejected.

When this policy is Disabled or Not Configured, file profiles are accepted whether or not they are signed.

This policy never restricts the built-in profiles (home, enterprise-workstation, and so on): they ship inside the product itself. Distribute your organisation's public key to the trust store — for example through this same Group Policy object or Intune — so that only profiles your administrators signed are honoured on managed devices.</string>
    </stringTable>
    <presentationTable />
  </resources>
</policyDefinitionResources>
